Message-ID: <loom.20121113T093035-30@post.gmane.org>

Simon Urbanek <simon.urbanek <at> r-project.org> writes:

> 
> Donald,
> 
> On Aug 29, 2010, at 11:26 AM, Donald Winston wrote:
> 
> > I'm pooling Rserve connections. 
> > 
> 
> Why on Earth would you do that?
> 
> > First is is it right to assume this is desirable or necessary?
> > 
> 
> I'm not sure what you mean exactly by pooling - but if you mean opening 
connections pre-emptively or
> re-using connections then, this is in general not desirable at all since most 
clients assume they have a
> new, clean connection with unpolluted workspace etc. Re-using connections is 
as dangerous as re-using
> needles - you never know what you get ;).
> 
> > There's no open() so I assume that new RConnection() "opens" a connection. 
Is this true?
> > 
> 
> You are again very vague - so I'm assuming you are talking about the REngine-
based Java client and in that
> case, the constructor creates a socket, connects to the server and performs 
the initial handshake. If you
> call that "open" then I suppose the answer is yes.
> 
> > Is it ok to call shutdown() without closing all the connections? (I need at 
least one to call shutdown()).
> > 
> 
> Shutdown signals the server to tear down its listening queue and terminate 
gracefully. It has no effect on
> any open client instances - I suppose you could call that ok. (Modulo control 
commands - the server will
> tear down its end of the control pipes so clients can no longer issue control 
commands, but that is quite
> logical as there is no server process anymore).
> 
> > What would I use close() for?
> > 
> 
> To close the connection and thus allow the associated child process to exit, 
remove temporary working
> directory etc.
> 
> > Kind of confusing because jdbc sql connections don't appear to work this 
way. (db pooling class I use
> handles all this stuff for me)
> 
> Rserve has pretty much nothing to do with JDBC so I fail to see any connection 
here...
> 
> Cheers,
> Simon
> 
> 

Hi 

As you said it is not desirable to re use Rserve connections, but what if Rserve 
will be a part of my web app and 1000s of users going to use it. In this case 
how to limit R connection, to not let the R be overloaded? Note: I am using 
REngine-based Java client.

And another question. As far as I understand each connection reserves its own 
workspace. What if for each session I set workspace to same directory (by 
invoking setwd(path))? May it encounter problems?

Thank you very much for response in advance.  
Frank

