> 
> Hi 
> 
> As you said it is not desirable to re use Rserve connections, but what if Rserve 
> will be a part of my web app and 1000s of users going to use it. In this case 
> how to limit R connection, to not let the R be overloaded? Note: I am using 
> REngine-based Java client.
> 

R won't be overloaded since each connection has its own R (although they share the initial image for efficiency).

There is an inherent limit on connections and that is imposed by the OS (# of FDs/sockets). There is no explicit limit of parallel processes in Rserve.

That said, you could have a pool of workers managed by your Java code. However, note that re-use of connections has consequences - you may need to clean up after each connection and run the garbage collector, otherwise your memory usage will rise unnecessarily. If you have a quick, fixed code it may be worth it, but note that subsequent connections can technically affect each other (e.g. if you allow custom code evaluation that may change options, use connections etc.).

I'm running Rserve as a back-end to web services on several production servers (mostly using FastRWeb) and have not seen a problem with scalability so far.


> And another question. As far as I understand each connection reserves its own 
> workspace. What if for each session I set workspace to same directory (by 
> invoking setwd(path))? May it encounter problems?
> 

Not as far as Rserve is concerned. You can encounter collisions if you are not careful what/how you write files, but that is true in general when accessing same files from multiple processes.


